Immortalist <sir_killalot_x@hotmail.com> wrote:
> Well, this is honestly the first time this has happened to me: my
> Barbarian was drowned by a Giant Eel. Now, the thing is that I got the
> message which said the Eel swinged itself around me.

Amen. I had a Valk die to that yesterday, and it was by far my best
game _ever_. I had a scroll of teleport which I could have read,
_if_ I'd known it was an immediate life-threatening emergency; but
by previous experience things which grab you (pythons, owlbears,
carnivorous apes etc) just squeeze you gently and give you time to
kill them by normal means. So I took one swipe at the eel, it didn't
die, I did, game over.

I'll know next time, fair enough. But it does seem a bit off that I
had no chance to respond between finding out it was a serious
emergency and finding out I was dead.

I mean, I also tackled the Quest for the first time in this game,
and found out the hard way that Lord Surtur could teleport himself
right next to me, meaning he attacked me on the 1/5 HP I had left
from fighting his retinue. But that wasn't an _instant_ death; I had
time to escalate through a few escape and healing mechanisms I'd
been prudently carrying about, and my thought after killing Surtur
was `phew, that was close, I'll know to be more careful of the quest
nemesis next time, and meanwhile I must go and replenish my assorted
escape equipment because having it around is obviously at least as
good an idea as I thought it was'.

As far as lessons learned the hard way goes, that's hard enough for
me; arbitrarily wasting a 16th-level character with DSM and full HP
in a single turn with no foreseeable recourse is a hell of a way to
tell you not to stand too near a moat.

> If a player hasn't been informed of such (and I have killed hundreds
> of those creatures before anyway)

Yeah, it would have been less bad if the _first_ eel I met had done
that to me. But since it was about the fifteenth, I'd put a lot more
effort into the character before the game bothered to teach me this
lesson...
